Z||000004||Dan86gt3.8||10-01-2000||11:31 AM||||terry, Its a 95 year model engine.  <BR>88formula its the series 2.<BR>Now I am reall interested to see what a 3.8 would do with a 4 or 5 speed. I guess one cool thing is that it shitfts into overdrive right after I cross the line.<P>fropuff, if you were looking to be real fast go with the 3.8 s/c if you are going for n/a I would go with the 3.4 tdc on a manual tranny, but to each his own I was just trying to fill your head with a little nonsense<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Shaun41178||10-02-2000||04:41 PM||||Glad to see you made it to the track Dan!  Those are some good times actually.  Those times will be able to hang with stock to mildly modified 305's and stock 350's  You can also surprise a lot of Mustang guys as many of them run low 10's in the 1/8th mile.  <P>Try working on getting that 60' time down.  You spinning at all?  You power brake it at all?  With my 2.8 and no nitrous I would power brake it and get 2.3 60' times as well.  Try power braking if you didn't do that.  You might knock off a .1 or so and that could get you a really low 15 or if lucky a high 14.  90 mph trap speed is good too.  That is what I run with my nitrous.<P>Its good to see what it will run everyday though.  Now you have a baseline and now if you want you can take all the nonessentials out for weight savings, and play with tire pressure and ice down the intake to see how much of a difference all that makes.  Planning on that shift improver or shift kit?<P>Also what elevation you at?  If above 1000' feet you can use the NHRA formula to see what it will do at sea level  I know you are in Waco but I don't know if that is at a high elevation or not.<P>Oh yea if you ever decide to go nitrous an easy on the engine 50 shot will get you into the low 14's and a 70 will get you into the 13's for sure.<P>Its a bummer to hear about that damn cv boot again.  I thought you had that fixed.  Hopedully it will stop giving you headaches.<P>See you online.<P>------------------<BR>Shaun-Toledo OH, 85 GT<BR>3.2 With nitrous<BR>http://members.aol.com/shaun41178||||reg||1
Z||000006||hugh||10-02-2000||08:04 PM||||In bracket racing consistency is the single most important thing.I don't know how much you have run,but if you start on the last yellow you'll have a better reaction time.||||reg||1
Z||000007||88formula||10-04-2000||09:52 PM||||Just for the hell of it I ran the numbers on Dyno2000 and Drag2000 to see what I would get. I guessed the camshaft specifications and got 200 hp @5500 rpm and 228 ft. lbs. of torque at 4000 rpm. On the drag program with the automatic four-speed transmission with a 3.08 final drive ratio a 2925 lb. fiero ran to 60 in 7.4 seconds and went the quarter mile in 15.391 seconds @ 93 mph. With the getdrag 5-speed those numbers drop to a 5.7 for 0-60 and 14 seconds in the 1/4 mile at 96 mph. ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Cooter||10-04-2000||10:28 PM||||hey 88- you are getting pretty good at that desktop drag racing! What would my 3.4 with the 4.10's do?||||reg||1
Z||000009||88formula||10-05-2000||05:01 PM||||If you have the stock 3.4, which is 165 horsepower with high performance manifolds and mufflers than your 2975 lb. fiero should run around 14.67 @ 91.4 mph in the quarter mile and 0-60 in 6.38 seconds. That is if you launch your car at 3500 rpm and shift at 6000 rpm and you have the five-speed transmission.<P>Am I close? ||||reg||1
Z||000010||Dan86gt3.8||10-05-2000||10:44 PM||||Well the dyno was right on for the time but the mph is off.  It ran 90.xx all 5 times.  I have a feeling it would run better with a getrag.  But I also bet if I could get a stall converter on the auto I would drop a couple tenths.  ||||reg||1
